stth wrote:
> Doug Freyburger wrote:
>
>  > I've played some knights walking my horse around and the things end
>  > up killing machines for their level. My first one attacked the mine
>  > town priest so I learned to close the door while I was using the
>  > altar.
>
> LOL! Yeah, they become quite ambitious #-).

Having such a powerful pet is a significant advantage for knights. 
Using a leash is wonderful but they aren't common in early games.

>  > Do you start jousting from the gate?  Lances occasionally break in
>  > jousting so I have wondered when to start using it.
>
> Riding at XL:1 can kill you if you fall off your steed while mounting
> (up to 20 HP loss), so I wait until I have enough HP to survive a fall
> with a safety margin. Around 30-35 HP or so.

I didn't think in terms of asking about riding, fighting while mounted
and jousting separately.  They can all be done separately so it makes
sense for all three to have separate decision points.

> Since ridden pets are more passive in combat and often take a beating
> from monsters, letting your pony grow up to be less vulnerable is also
> important. Horses also get speedier while they grow up, so my goal is
> to let it grow into a warhorse ASAP. So I regularly dismount and let
> my horse kill monsters.

Using a leash to dance a horse around the watchmen gives a good supply
of gloves and other items.  Depending on how much combat you've given
your pet horses they tend to be able to take out the watch captain on
the way down. Bingo a spare sword (dipping plus crowning to give a
spare rustproof weapon) or saber.  Other pet types need to wait until
the way back up to take out the watch captain.

I had been walking my horse all the way through mines end and back up
before riding.  I've done few riding games over time and mostly played
classes other than knights.  Start riding as soon as your pet upgrades
to war horse.  That makes sense - Traditionally ponies have been for
pack, horses for travel, war horses for combat.

> As for the lance, I usually only apply it until I can increase luck.
> Applying doesn't trigger passive monster attacks, so it's a great way
> to dispatch floating eyes and jellies safely. You have to be careful
> with peacefuls though, because you don't get the warning message you
> get in melee.
>
> Once I have a luckstone (for it's +3 luck), I start to use the lance
> for jousting. This seems to work so far. Of course, I usually try to
> maximise luck as soon as possible then.

Is there a non-joust melee for the lance?  The times I've tried it has
only worked like other poleaxes.  I hit with the hilt if if it were a
weak stick.  Or is applying it while not riding not jousting so it's
like using a poleam.

> I actually remember only two lance breakings. Both even happened in
> the same game. One was rather early (with low luck), the other after
> the Castle (IIRC; maxed luck). Luckily, I found spares in the Castle
> and (I think) Fort Ludios. Because I generally dip for Excalibur with
> Knights, I had a good backup weapon, but seeing that +6 lance break
> was really annoying #-).

While walking I do use long sword for melee plus whatever missiles make
sense at the time.  I've used lance walking, but because of the starting
long sword and guaranteed Excalibur by either dipping or crowning I've
wanted to run it up to skilled ASAP and expert much later.

Because I use corridors a lot to avoid crowds of monsters I seem to use
my alternate weapon Excalibur more than the lance.

Expert at long sword, lance and riding, basic at dagger and/or bow,
skilled at all three spell schools and two weapon by the late game.  I
like to run dagger up to basic as early as I can to have a missile
option and because daggers quivered don't count as a wield, then lance
and long sword up to skilled using "x" to switch back and forth. Then
riding skills mixed with any other enhancement order to reach the
final goal of maxing those out.

Since Excalibur is an artifact, there are no artifact lances, and
knights can two weapon I wonder about doing a lot of jousting.  Not an
option in dark or maze levels.  Great for both castles.  Is it that much
better than dual wield of Excalibur and a nice non-artifact melee weapon
most of the time?


